# Trait Design Practices & Code Smells

## Small traits compose; big traits rot

```rust
// BAD: one trait to rule everything
trait App { fn render(); fn save(); fn auth(); fn http(); }

// GOOD: capabilities callers actually request
trait Renderable { fn render(&self) -> String; }
trait Persistable { fn save(&self) -> Result<(), std::io::Error>; }
```

Every extra required method is a tax on implementors and a hint you modeled a
*role*, not a capability.

## The newtype pattern

Wrap primitives to encode meaning and gain trait implementations safely:

```rust
struct Port(u16);
struct Email(String);

impl Port {
    fn parse(raw: &str) -> Result<Self, String> {
        let value: u16 = raw.parse().map_err(|_| "invalid port")?;
        if value == 0 { return Err("port 0 reserved".into()); }
        Ok(Self(value))
    }
}
```

Now `Port` and `u16` cannot be swapped by accident — the type system carries
your domain rules.

## Smell: God structs

A struct with fifteen fields mixing config, cache and business state resists
testing and reasoning. Split by responsibility; pass the pieces each function
actually needs.

## Smell: trait bloat / needless generics

If only one implementation will ever exist, a trait is ceremony. If a generic
parameter appears exactly once in a signature, consider concrete types. Add
abstraction at the second use case, not the imagined third.

## Composition over inheritance-style thinking

Rust has no subclassing. Build small parts and `struct`-compose them:
`struct Service { repo: SqlRepo, clock: SystemClock }` beats deep hierarchies.

examples.code:
#[derive(Debug)]
struct Port(u16);

impl Port {
    fn parse(raw: &str) -> Result<Port, String> {
        let value: u16 = raw.parse().map_err(|_| String::from("invalid port"))?;
        if value == 0 {
            return Err(String::from("port 0 is reserved"));
        }
        Ok(Port(value))
    }
}

fn main() {
    println!("{:?}", Port::parse("8080"));
    println!("{:?}", Port::parse("nope"));
}

exercise.starterCode:
fn handle(raw: &str) {
    let n: u64 = raw.parse().unwrap_or(8080);
    if n > 65535 {
        println!("bad");
    } else if n == 0 {
        println!("bad");
    } else {
        println!("port ok {n}");
    }
}

fn main() {
    for raw in ["80", "99999", "x"] {
        handle(raw);
    }
}

exercise.solution:
fn parse_port(raw: &str) -> Result<u16, String> {
    let port = raw.parse::<u16>().map_err(|_| format!("invalid port: {raw}"))?;
    if port == 0 {
        return Err(String::from("port 0 is reserved"));
    }
    Ok(port)
}

fn main() {
    for raw in ["80", "99999", "x"] {
        match parse_port(raw) {
            Ok(port) => println!("port ok {port}"),
            Err(err) => println!("{err}"),
        }
    }
}
